{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "final_layout_keyframe",
      "description": "After the first requested container was lifted, returned and released, the robot obscured the next requested container in the front view while that container was outside the wrist view. The stored target_locations correctly identified it, and completed_lifts correctly selected it, but the policy estimated an inaccurate approach position and yaw. This breaks SUFFICIENCY: a silhouette-centre identity does not preserve the hidden top-face geometry. Add a field holding the last demonstration frame showing the settled final arrangement with the requested containers unobstructed. Set it at the video cycle and retain it unchanged throughout execution. It preserves geometry for still-unmoved targets, not the current geometry of returned containers. Example value: V113.",
      "value_type": "keyframe"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "final_layout_keyframe",
      "description": "When approaching a later requested container after returning the first, the robot can obscure the target in the front camera while it lies outside the wrist camera. The identity list and completion count remain correct, but they cannot supply its hidden grasp geometry, breaking SUFFICIENCY. During the video cycle, select the last shown frame of the settled final arrangement in which the requested containers are unobstructed, and write its label as final_layout_keyframe, for example V113. Carry that label unchanged at every act cycle. Do not replace it with a robot-occluded execution frame or update it when a completed container is returned."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "The failures occurred when the open hand had withdrawn after returning the first requested container and was approaching the second. The identity list and completion count selected the correct target, but the predicted horizontal approach positions were wrong: the target could be clipped in the wrist view, occluded in the front view, or outside the wrist view altogether. These are geometry-related SUFFICIENCY failures, not evidence for changing target identities or completion transitions. Extend the existing calibrated reconstruction rule as follows. For an unfinished, unmoved target, use final_layout_keyframe as an unobstructed reference whenever current views hide or truncate its physical top corners. Match the reference container using target_locations; do not treat that stored silhouette centre as its grasp-axis projection. Fit the horizontal square top and visible vertical sides using the calibrated reference front view together with any usable current observations. Recover height from consistent geometric evidence rather than choosing an arbitrary projection plane. In a clipped view, use visible physical edges and their square constraints to infer missing corners; the image boundary is not an object edge, and the centroid of the visible fragment is not the grasp centre. If the current wrist view provides no usable target geometry, do not force a wrist-based estimate: use the unobstructed reference geometry for the stationary target. Average reconstructed physical top corners for x,y and infer yaw from reconstructed world-plane edges. Use this centre for the open approach at z=0.20 m, then refine from current calibrated views before closing at z=0.0633 m. Do not use the final demonstration geometry as the current pose of a container that has since been moved. Keep the existing lift, return, withdrawal and completion rules unchanged. The additional inaccurate second-target approach for which no missing information was reported supports applying this same geometry rule consistently; it does not justify a separate memory field or a target-specific waypoint."
}

